WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
It's a new day on SpaceX Daily, episode 4, coming to you from Vancouver.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.620
It's June 15th, 2026.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.420
Let's get into today's SpaceX developments.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.920
Falcon 9's first flight after the NASDAQ debut tests whether public markets alter

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.660
the company's launch tempo at all.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:21.300
The mission marks the first Falcon 9 flight since the June 12 listing,

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:26.180
and continues the booster reuse campaign that has driven annual cadence gains.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:31.000
SpaceX has not disclosed the specific booster serial or target orbit,

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.280
but the launch returns the vehicle family to flight less than 72 hours after the IPO closed.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.220
Observers will watch pad turnaround time

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:44.080
and whether the flight rate holds steady through the transition to public reporting.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:50.420
Next milestone to track is the booster's post-flight inspection and potential rapid reflight window.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:56.200
This keeps the Falcon program moving forward on the same reuse cadence tracked last episode,

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.800
with no visible shift yet from the listing.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:04.740
Construction at the South Texas site continues to expand manufacturing and launch infrastructure,

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:10.400
even as nearby residents report vibration from ongoing static fire and test operations.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.180
The facility now supports parallel Starship Vehicle Assembly and Raptor Engine Work,

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.740
directly addressing the production-rate bottleneck that has limited flight test tempo.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.240
Local infrastructure upgrades remain tied to the same timeline as booster catch hardware maturation.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.260
The listing was executed from the Texas site,

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.140
and earmarked proceeds for expanded vehicle manufacturing lines and engine development.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:41.840
Capital allocation targets the same facilities already supporting parallel Starship and Falcon operations.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.740
No specific dollar figures for individual programs were released in the coverage.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:52.780
A Reddit user discovered that the SpaceX Ocean Rocket Recovery Crane is stored in a long

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:57.820
beach neighborhood and posted a photo of the equipment. The crane supports ocean recovery

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.600
operations that enable booster reuse on high energy trajectories.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.640
Its presence in a public storage area offers a rare ground level view of the logistics chain

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:13.320
behind Falcon and future Starship recovery. Reddit users are circulating photos of the

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:19.280
long beach recovery crane and debating its exact lift capacity for future Starship class boosters.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:25.900
Observers near starbase continue to log daily vibration reports tied to ongoing engine tests

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:30.320
and note incremental facility expansions visible from public roads.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:37.200
Local aerospace forums are tracking the first post-IPO Falcon 9 countdown for any visible

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.800
changes in range safety or FAA coordination cadence. Space enthusiasts are sharing archival

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:49.340
footage of earlier ocean recoveries to contextualize the hardware now sitting in a residential neighborhood.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.880
From an engineering standpoint, the ocean recovery crane photographed in long beach

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.480
is the physical embodiment of a first principle's decision to recover the highest value hardware

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.320
rather than treat the booster as expendable.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:09.400
Raw material cost for a Falcon 9 first stage is dominated by the aluminum lithium

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:15.040
tankage and nine merlin engines. Even at generous scrap plus labor estimates,

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.120
the stage itself is worth far less than the price of building a new one.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.240
By designing a crane and support ship system that can safely retrieve that stage from the open ocean,

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:30.840
SpaceX collapses the effective cost per flight from the full multiple of a new build stage

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.880
down to the marginal cost of inspection, refurbishment, and reflight.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.540
The same logic scales directly to starship once tower or ocean catch hardware matures,

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:46.500
dropping the marginal cost of each additional flight toward propellant and ground operations only.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:53.020
Blue origins new Glenn rocket exploded on the launch pad during a recent test.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.440
The explosion removes a near-term heavy lift option from upcoming manifests.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:03.420
The incident directly affects NASA lunar timelines that had counted on new Glenn for at least

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:09.440
one Artemis-related cargo flight. Resolution depends on the length of the accident investigation.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.560
Blue origin must still determine whether it can meet its revised 2026 flight target.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.360
On the AI front, SpaceX shifted Colossus 1 capacity to Anthropic after internal

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:27.860
rock workloads encountered latency issues. The Memphis cluster is being treated as shared

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:34.120
infrastructure across the Musk ecosystem. The move highlights how compute originally tied to XAI

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:40.840
is now being allocated to external partners. SpaceX continues orbital data center development

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:46.760
in parallel with the shift. A special feature notes that IPO proceeds are being eyed for both

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:51.900
terrestrial and orbital AI infrastructure buildouts. And a quick market note,

00:04:52.340 --> 00:05:00.800
SPCX closed at $160.95. Before we go, the next post-flight inspection window on this Falcon 9

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.740
will show whether the rapid reflight cadence holds after the listing.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:15.060
That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today. SPCX closed at $160.95. A quick rating on

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:20.520
Apple Podcasts or Spotify goes a long way. I'm Patrick and Vancouver, see you tomorrow.

